let __v_31 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"paprika\",\"sweet paprika\",\"hungarian paprika\"]},\"Paprika is mild, around 1,000 Scoville - closer to a red pepper than to a chilli. A teaspoon colours a pot and sweetens it more than it heats it, which is why a goulash can take two spoons and still be gentle.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"smoked paprika\",\"smoky paprika\",\"pimenton\"]},\"Smoked paprika is mild too, around 1,000 Scoville - the smoke comes from drying the peppers over oak, not from heat. Half a teaspoon is enough to taste it; more turns a dish bitter rather than hotter.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cayenne\",\"cayenne pepper\"]},\"Cayenne is hot: about 30,000 to 50,000 Scoville, so a quarter teaspoon heats a whole pot. It has little taste of its own, which is why it is the thing to reach for when a dish needs heat and not another flavour.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chilli powder\",\"chili powder\"]},\"Chilli powder is a blend, usually mild to medium with cumin and oregano in it, so the heat depends on the jar - some are mostly cayenne. Taste a pinch on your tongue before you decide how much goes in.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chilli flakes\",\"chili flakes\",\"red pepper flakes\",\"crushed chillies\"]},\"Chilli flakes are medium-hot, around 30,000 to 50,000 Scoville, and they give their heat slowly: a pinch in oil at the start is warmer than the same pinch scattered at the end.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"curry powder\"]},\"Curry powder is mild - it is turmeric, cumin, coriander and fenugreek, and it is not where the heat in a curry comes from. Two spoons in a curry for four is about right, and the heat is added separately with chilli.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"garam masala\"]},\"Garam masala is warm, not hot: cinnamon, cardamom, cloves and pepper, ground together. A teaspoon goes in at the end of cooking, where the smell survives; boiled for an hour it tastes of dust.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cumin\",\"jeera\"]},\"Cumin is not hot at all - it is warm, earthy and slightly bitter, and it is the smell of a curry rather than the fire in it. A teaspoon for four, fried in the oil for a few seconds first.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"turmeric\",\"haldi\"]},\"Turmeric is not hot: earthy and a little bitter, and too much of it tastes of soap. Half a teaspoon for four is plenty, and it stains a wooden spoon and a white shirt for good.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cinnamon\",\"cassia\"]},\"Cinnamon is warm and sweet, not hot. The sticks sold cheaply are cassia, which is stronger and slightly bitter - fine in a curry, and worth knowing if a recipe came out tasting of cloves.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"black pepper\",\"peppercorns\",\"ground pepper\"]},\"Black pepper is mildly hot, at the back of the throat, and nothing like chilli: a whole teaspoon of it is gentler than a pinch of cayenne. Freshly ground is far sharper than the dust in a jar.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"white pepper\"]},\"White pepper is hotter than black, with a farmyard smell that suits soups, congee and Chinese cooking. Use half as much as you would black.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mustard powder\",\"english mustard\",\"mustard\"]},\"Mustard is hot in a different place from chilli - it burns in the nose and fades in a few seconds. Mixed with water it is hottest; vinegar or hot liquid takes the edge off it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ginger\",\"root ginger\",\"ginger powder\"]},\"Ginger is warm and sharp rather than hot like chilli, and the burn fades in seconds. A thumb-sized piece grated into a curry for four is a good amount.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"garlic powder\",\"garlic granules\"]},\"Garlic powder is not hot at all - it is savoury and a little sweet, and it burns if it sits in hot oil, so add it with the liquid.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"coriander\",\"coriander seed\",\"ground coriander\"]},\"Coriander seed is not hot: it tastes of orange peel and hay, and it is the rounded body in a curry powder. Toast it before grinding and the smell doubles.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"nutmeg\"]},\"Nutmeg is warm and sweet, not hot - a quarter teaspoon in a cheese sauce or a pudding is the usual dose, and more tastes of medicine.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cloves\",\"ground cloves\"]},\"Cloves are warm and very strong, not hot, and two or three in a pot is enough. Too many and the dish tastes of the dentist.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cardamom\"]},\"Cardamom is aromatic, not hot: menthol and flowers. Crush the pods just before they go in, because the seeds lose their smell within a day of grinding.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"five spice\",\"chinese five spice\"]},\"Five spice is not hot - star anise, fennel, cinnamon, cloves and pepper - and it is strong, so half a teaspoon for four is the usual amount.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sumac\"]},\"Sumac is not hot: it is sour and fruity, the taste of lemon without the wet. A teaspoon over a salad or grilled meat at the end.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"horseradish\",\"wasabi\"]},\"Horseradish is hot in the nose, like mustard, and the burn lasts seconds rather than minutes. A teaspoon of the creamed sort is plenty for a plate of beef.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sriracha\"]},\"Sriracha is mild to medium, around 2,000 Scoville - ketchup-hot rather than chilli-hot, and sweet. A spoon per bowl adds heat slowly enough to taste as you go.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tabasco\",\"tabasco sauce\"]},\"Tabasco is about 2,500 Scoville, sharper and much saltier than sriracha, so a few shakes per plate. It is vinegar-forward: added early it dulls, added at the table it does not.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"gochujang\"]},\"Gochujang is medium, around 1,000 to 2,000 Scoville, and sweet and fermented with it, so the heat arrives after the sweetness. A tablespoon for four.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"harissa\"]},\"Harissa is medium-hot, roughly 4,000 to 5,000 Scoville, with cumin and garlic in the paste. A spoon per portion stirred into the sauce at the end.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"jalapeno\",\"jalapenos\",\"jalapeño\"]},\"A jalapeño is mild to medium, 2,500 to 8,000 Scoville: hot enough to eat raw in slices, and much milder pickled or cooked. The seeds and white pith hold most of the heat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"serrano\",\"serranos\"]},\"A serrano is 10,000 to 23,000 Scoville - two or three times a jalapeño, with a cleaner, greener taste. Half a one is enough for a curry for four.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"poblano\",\"poblanos\"]},\"A poblano is mild, 1,000 to 1,500 Scoville - roasted and stuffed it tastes of smoke and green pepper, not of heat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ancho\",\"anchos\"]},\"An ancho is a dried poblano: mild, 1,000 to 2,000 Scoville, raisin-sweet and a little smoky. Two soaked in a sauce for four give colour and depth without heat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chipotle\",\"chipotles\",\"chipotle in adobo\"]},\"A chipotle is a smoked, dried jalapeño, 2,500 to 8,000 Scoville. The adobo it comes in is milder than the pepper itself - one pepper and a spoon of the sauce for a pot for four.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"habanero\",\"habaneros\",\"habañero\"]},\"A habanero is very hot, 100,000 to 350,000 Scoville, and fruity with it. A quarter of one heats a pot for four, and it is worth wearing gloves for a batch: the oil stays on hands for hours and soap barely shifts it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"scotch bonnet\",\"scotch bonnets\"]},\"A scotch bonnet is very hot, 100,000 to 350,000 Scoville, with a fruity, almost apricot taste that survives the heat. One, deseeded, is enough for a family curry.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"allspice\"]},\"Allspice is warm and peppery, not hot - it tastes of cinnamon, clove and nutmeg at once, which is where the name comes from. A few berries in a stew or a cake, and no heat at all.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"star anise\"]},\"Star anise is not hot: it is liquorice-sweet and very strong, so one star in a pot for four. Two and the dish tastes of cough sweets.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"fennel seed\",\"fennel seeds\",\"fennel\"]},\"Fennel seed is not hot - it is sweet and aniseedy, and it is half the taste of a sausage. A teaspoon crushed in a curry for four.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"fenugreek\",\"methi\"]},\"Fenugreek is not hot: it is bitter and maple-sweet, and a little goes a long way. The leaves are gentler than the seeds, which turn a dish bitter if they are fried too hard.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"asafoetida\",\"hing\"]},\"Asafoetida is not hot, but it is ferociously strong - a pinch in hot oil, no more, and it smells of onions and garlic once it has cooked. A quarter teaspoon for a pot for four.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"curry leaves\",\"curry leaf\"]},\"Curry leaves are not hot at all: they are citrusy and nutty, and they only give that up when fried in hot oil for a few seconds at the start. Dried ones taste of very little.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"zaatar\",\"za'atar\"]},\"Za'atar is not hot: it is thyme, sesame and sumac, so it is herby and sour. A spoon over bread or yoghurt, not a source of heat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"berbere\"]},\"Berbere is medium to hot, roughly 3,000 to 5,000 Scoville - a blend of chilli, paprika, fenugreek and warm spices. A tablespoon for four gives an Ethiopian stew its heat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"jerk seasoning\",\"jerk spice\",\"jamaican jerk\"]},\"Jerk seasoning is hot: it is scotch bonnet with allspice, thyme and sugar, so it can be 100,000 Scoville or more. A tablespoon per kilo of chicken, and less if the jar is old - chilli loses its heat over a year or two.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"peri peri\",\"piri piri\"]},\"Peri peri is hot, 50,000 to 175,000 Scoville, and sharp with lemon and vinegar. A spoon in a marinade for four is a strong heat once it is cooked down.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"sambal\",\"sambal oelek\"]},\"Sambal is medium to hot, 2,000 to 10,000 Scoville depending on the jar, and salty and sour with it. A teaspoon per portion stirred in at the end.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ras el hanout\"]},\"Ras el hanout is a Moroccan blend and not usually hot - cumin, coriander, cinnamon, ginger and pepper - though some jars have chilli in. A tablespoon for a tagine or a tray of vegetables.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"togarashi\",\"shichimi togarashi\",\"nanami togarashi\"]},\"Togarashi is mild to medium, mostly sesame, orange peel and seaweed with a little chilli, so it is a finishing sprinkle rather than a heat source.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chilli oil\",\"chili oil\",\"chili crisp\",\"chilli crisp\"]},\"Chilli oil is as hot as the chillies that went into it - usually medium, around 10,000 Scoville - and the crisp bits are hotter than the oil. A teaspoon over noodles.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"hot sauce\"]},\"Hot sauce covers everything from 500 to 300,000 Scoville, so the bottle is the only way to know: Tabasco is about 2,500, sriracha about 2,000, and a scotch bonnet sauce can be a hundred times hotter. Taste a drop before you commit.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"birds eye\",\"bird's eye\",\"bird eye chilli\",\"thai chilli\"]},\"A bird's eye chilli is hot, 50,000 to 100,000 Scoville - one or two will heat a curry for four, and they are hotter dried than fresh.\"]}]}")?;
